# MCSP Semantic Definitions — 0.5

**Status:** Constructive implementation  
**Qualification:** Under Conditional Experiment  
**Goal:** Reduce 0.4 semantic residue and make the definition mechanism recursively representable.

## Core representation

MCSP provisionally treats every representable entity as a CONSTRUCT.

    CONSTRUCT {
      identity
      kind
      relations
      state
    }

A definition is itself a construct:

    DEFINE {
      identity
      kind
      inputs
      outputs
      preconditions
      transition
      outcomes
      relations
      realization_constraints
    }

Therefore DEFINE is not a privileged external metalanguage. Its fields must themselves resolve to constructs and relations.

## Foundational candidates

    DEFINE IDENTITY {
      kind: relation
      inputs: construct
      outputs: identity
      transition: distinguish construct within a declared scope
    }

    DEFINE RELATION {
      kind: construct
      inputs: source, target, relation-kind
      outputs: related-state
      transition: associate source and target under relation-kind
    }

    DEFINE POSITION {
      kind: relation
      inputs: construct, space, coordinates
      outputs: position
      transition: relate construct to a position within space
    }

    DEFINE VALUE {
      kind: construct
      inputs: representation, width
      outputs: value
      transition: represent distinguishable state under width
    }

    DEFINE WIDTH {
      kind: relation
      inputs: value-or-space, unit, extent
      outputs: width
      transition: relate an extent to a declared unit
    }

    DEFINE STATE {
      kind: construct
      inputs: construct, observations
      outputs: state
      transition: represent observable properties of construct at a logical observation position
    }

    DEFINE TRANSITION {
      kind: relation
      inputs: pre-state, operation, constraints
      outputs: post-state, outcome
      transition: relate pre-state to post-state under operation and constraints
    }

    DEFINE CONDITION {
      kind: construct
      inputs: relation, observations
      outputs: condition
      transition: represent the evaluated state of a relation
    }

    DEFINE ATTRIBUTE {
      kind: relation
      inputs: construct, attribute-kind, value
      outputs: attributed-construct
      transition: relate a declared property to a construct
    }

    DEFINE CONSTRAINT {
      kind: relation
      inputs: construct-or-transition, requirement
      outputs: constrained-relation
      transition: restrict admissible states or transitions
    }

    DEFINE SPACE {
      kind: construct
      inputs: identity, relations
      outputs: space
      transition: establish a positional relation domain
    }

## Machine-operation candidates

    DEFINE COPY {
      kind: transition
      inputs: source, destination, width
      outputs: destination-state
      transition: destination value becomes source value under width
    }

    DEFINE TRANSLATE {
      kind: transition
      inputs: position, source-space, target-space, mapping
      outputs: target-position
      transition: derive corresponding target-space position
      outcomes: completed | exception | unsupported
    }

    DEFINE READ {
      kind: transition
      inputs: position, width, attributes
      outputs: destination-state
      relations: TRANSLATE
      transition: observe addressed state and transfer its value to destination
    }

    DEFINE WRITE {
      kind: transition
      inputs: position, source, width, attributes
      outputs: addressed-state
      relations: TRANSLATE
      transition: change addressed state using source value subject to constraints
    }

    DEFINE COMPUTE {
      kind: transition
      inputs: operation, values, width, attributes
      outputs: destination-state
      transition: apply operation relation to inputs
    }

    DEFINE COMPARE {
      kind: transition
      inputs: relation, values, width
      outputs: condition
      transition: evaluate relation over values
    }

    DEFINE SCHEDULE {
      kind: relation
      inputs: transitions, ordering, constraints
      outputs: execution-order
      transition: establish admissible temporal/logical ordering
    }

    DEFINE ALLOCATE {
      kind: transition
      inputs: resource-space, extent, constraints
      outputs: allocation-relation
      transition: associate an available region/resource with a construct
    }

    DEFINE RELEASE {
      kind: transition
      inputs: allocation-relation
      outputs: resource-state
      transition: terminate allocation relation subject to constraints
    }

    DEFINE TRANSFER {
      kind: transition
      inputs: source, destination, extent, constraints
      outputs: destination-state, outcome
      transition: move or reproduce represented state across positions/spaces
    }

    DEFINE SIGNAL {
      kind: transition
      inputs: source, target, event
      outputs: target-observable-state
      transition: make event state observable to target
    }

    DEFINE BRANCH {
      kind: transition
      inputs: condition, target, fallthrough
      outputs: control-position
      transition: select next control position from condition
    }

    DEFINE INVOKE {
      kind: transition
      inputs: target, continuation, convention
      outputs: control-position, continuation-relation
      transition: select target control position and preserve continuation relation
    }

    DEFINE RETURN {
      kind: transition
      inputs: continuation, convention
      outputs: control-position
      transition: select control position from continuation relation
    }

## Outcome model

    DEFINE OUTCOME {
      kind: construct
      inputs: transition, result-kind, state
      outputs: outcome
      transition: classify observed completion state
    }

Initial result kinds:

    completed
    exception
    unsupported
    implementation-defined
    environment-defined
    incomplete

These labels are classifications, not assumptions that every host exposes identical exception machinery.

## Operation model

An OPERATION is a construct related to a TRANSITION rule.

    DEFINE OPERATION {
      kind: construct
      inputs: identity, input-relations, output-relations, transition-rule
      outputs: operation
      transition: establish executable semantic relation
    }

This allows ADD, AND, comparison relations, protocol actions and device commands to be described as operation constructs rather than privileged language keywords.

## Exception model

    DEFINE EXCEPTION {
      kind: outcome
      inputs: transition, cause, observable-state
      outputs: exception
      transition: classify a transition that did not produce its normal completed post-state
    }

Host trap vectors, exception levels, fault codes and handlers are realization mappings.

## Convention model

    DEFINE CONVENTION {
      kind: relation
      inputs: participating-constructs, rules
      outputs: convention
      transition: establish agreed constraints without changing fundamental semantics
    }

Calling conventions, ABIs and protocol conventions therefore remain explicit relations rather than universal machine laws.

## Realization model

    DEFINE REALIZATION {
      kind: relation
      inputs: mcsp-construct, host, host-capabilities, mapping
      outputs: host-realization
      transition: relate stable MCSP semantics to concrete host mechanisms
    }

    DEFINE CAPABILITY {
      kind: relation
      inputs: host, construct-or-feature
      outputs: supported | unsupported | constrained
      transition: classify host realization availability
    }

## Recursive definition test

The definition form can now be restated in its own terms:

    DEFINE is a CONSTRUCT.
    each DEFINE field is a RELATION from the defined CONSTRUCT.
    inputs and outputs are RELATION sets.
    preconditions and realization_constraints are CONSTRAINT relations.
    transition is a TRANSITION relation.
    outcomes are OUTCOME constructs.
    identity is an IDENTITY relation.
    kind is a RELATION to another CONSTRUCT.

This removes the 0.4 assumption that DEFINE must remain an external privileged schema.

## Remaining residue

The following are still not closed and require further decomposition:

- representation
- observation
- unit
- extent
- scope
- coordinates
- mapping
- ordering
- requirement
- event
- rule
- cause
- logical observation position
- availability

The existence of this residue means MCSP self-description is **not yet closed**.

## 0.5 closure result

**PASS:** DEFINE can provisionally describe DEFINE structurally using CONSTRUCT and RELATION.

**NOT YET QUALIFIED:** the primitive vocabulary has not reached finite semantic closure because the remaining residue above still carries meaning not yet expressed in MCSP.

Next: decompose representation/observation/extent/ordering/mapping, then test whether the residue shrinks or merely renames external assumptions.
